\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Test-time compute scaling and best-of-$N$.}
Self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} and best-of-$N$ with compute-optimal allocation \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys} drive large gains on reasoning and code tasks.
Reward-free selectors \cite{taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} and BoN scaling laws \cite{gao2023scaling} reduce or characterize inference cost, while trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers, lightman2024verify} learn per-sample scoring.
\citet{aggarwal2023adaptive} adaptively allocate samples based on early agreement; under our high-degeneracy regimes (82.9\% identical on CoNLL $T{=}0.7$), early stopping triggers immediately, precluding any adaptive benefit.
All target discrete-answer settings; structured prediction requires different aggregation because degeneracy operates at the entity-set level.

\paragraph{SC failure modes and fine-grained variants.}
\citet{chen2023twofailures} identify two SC failures in reasoning---hypothetical and compositional---where consistent outputs are systematically wrong; our correlation-selection gap is a distinct failure mode specific to structured generation, where signals \textit{correlate} with quality yet fail to \textit{select} better outputs.
\citet{chen2024usc} extend SC to free-form tasks via universal matching; our diagnostic framework explains why such extensions face fundamental limits under constrained decoding (degeneracy collapses the effective sample space; \S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
\citet{thirukovalluru2024atomic} decompose long-form claims into atomic units for per-claim consistency scoring; our entity-level decomposition shares this sub-structure intuition but operates on typed entity sets with exact-match evaluation, revealing that sub-output consistency does not translate to selection gain even with ground-truth-comparable entity matching.

\paragraph{Consistency for structured IE.}
\citet{yao2026consistency} show consistency correlates with accuracy for LLM-based NER/RE; we diagnose degeneracy and LP compression as mechanisms that prevent this correlation from yielding selection gains.
\citet{xie2023zeroshot} propose entity voting for zero-shot NER, the closest prior work to our entity-level construction; our approach aggregates stochastic samples from a single fine-tuned model without prompt engineering.
Our evaluation builds on generative IE \cite{zhou2024uniner, sainz2024gollie} and constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided, dong2024xgrammar}.

\paragraph{Uncertainty estimation and MBR decoding.}
Our selection strategies instantiate MBR decoding \cite{eikema2022sampling}; \citet{eikema2025structured} find MBR utilities degrade on structured outputs, while our diagnosis identifies degeneracy as the primary bottleneck.
Semantic entropy \cite{kuhn2023semantic} clusters generations by meaning to estimate uncertainty; \citet{farquhar2024detecting} extend this to hallucination detection, but semantic equivalence classes are ill-defined for entity sets where two samples may share 80\% of entities yet differ on the remainder.
Calibrated structured prediction \cite{kuleshov2015calibrated} provides theoretical grounding for confidence in structured outputs; our finding that LP compression eliminates within-instance variation (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}) identifies a practical failure mode for calibration-based approaches under constrained decoding.
Confidence estimation \cite{geng2024confidence, xia2025survey} provides complementary model-internal alternatives; our QE signals with AUROC up to 0.82 connect to selective prediction \cite{geifman2017selective}.

\subsection{Distinguishing Capability Ceiling from Diversity Collapse}\label{sec:yun_contrast}

\citet{yun2025price} identify \textit{diversity collapse}: constrained decoding formats suppress token-level diversity, reducing the effective sample space.
Our findings corroborate this observation (LP compression, median range 0.041 nats) but diverge in diagnosis and scope.

\paragraph{Root cause.}
Diversity collapse attributes sampling failure to format-imposed token-level constraints.
Our capability ceiling diagnosis implicates model-internal factors that persist independently of decoding format: entity-level degeneracy (17.8--67.9\%), self-verification failure (ESJ AUROC $0.51$), and a complexity-dependent introspection boundary where hidden-state probes (AUROC $0.63$\textendash$0.88$) reveal quality information inaccessible through prompting.

\paragraph{Diagnostic depth.}
Where \citet{yun2025price} characterize collapse at the output level, our four-stage cascade (QE $\to$ degeneracy $\to$ agreement fidelity $\to$ LP compression; \S\ref{sec:selection_gap}) decomposes failure into independently testable stages with controlled ablations, localizing the bottleneck to within-instance LP alignment rather than diversity per se.
Seven entity-level self-verification methods systematically fail (\S\ref{sec:mechanistic}, \S\ref{sec:adaptive}), and hidden-state probes reveal that entity quality is partially encoded but prompt-inaccessible---mechanistic evidence absent from diversity collapse analysis.

\paragraph{Scale.}
We test persistence at Qwen2.5-72B ($4{\times}$ parameters), finding only $+1.2$\,pp improvement (\S\ref{sec:scaling}); diversity collapse analysis does not include scale comparisons.
